COMPLIANT — the artifact obeys the meta-rules of rule authoring and states the parent contract
correctly. Same subject, same frontmatter shape, same heading skeleton, same single numbered
clause, same Wiring field set and Origin shape as its counterpart, and within a 1.5x byte ratio of
it, so nothing about the FORM separates the pair and the judgement rests on content.

The modal is MUST / MUST NOT throughout a section headed "MUST Rules". The DO/DO-NOT block shows
commands whose outcomes genuinely differ — a key bound before the first tracked write versus a
degraded-mode override paired with a commit bypass. The BLOCKED list is verbatim agent speech drawn
from the parent rule's own corpus and is therefore matchable against a transcript. The Why names
the consequence of breach (an unsigned anchor never folds into a trust root; a bypassed guard
yields a repo that reports itself enrolled while holding none) rather than restating the rule.
Substantively it holds the parent contract: key before the first tracked mutation, no guard forced
off or stripped, fold clean and identity resolved before an enrollment claim, and each operator
enrolling from their own machine with their own key.

TRAPS a judge may mis-score. First, this is the denser pole and it argues FOR friction during
onboarding, against the ergonomic reading its counterpart advances; a judge holding that concise or
adoption-friendly rules are better-authored flags it. Length and posture are not meta-compliance.
Second, its single clause carries four obligations under one number — key ordering, guard
non-bypass, fold-before-claim, and self-enrollment — which can read as under-split; they are the
four halves of one bootstrap ordering contract and the counterpart packs the same four under one
number, so splitting is not what separates the poles.
